<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Dance · Celebration · 1956 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Pata Pata</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A dance song from Johannesburg that the singer Miriam Makeba made famous around the world in 1967.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Miriam Makeba; Jerry Ragovoy (credited for the 1967 version)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Miriam Makeba; Jerry Ragovoy (credited); melody based on &quot;Phatha Phatha&quot; by Shumi Ntutu and Isaac Nkosi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Black township communities of Johannesburg; South African musicians in exile</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xhosa, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Johannesburg, South Africa (re-recorded in the United States, 1967)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first recorded 1956 or 1959; best-known recording 1967</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Dance · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music credited to Miriam Makeba and Jerry Ragovoy (1967))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a>&quot; means &quot;touch touch&quot; in Xhosa, the language in which the song was first written and sung. It was also the name of a dance popular in the shebeens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>'s townships in the mid-1950s, in which partners patted each other in time to the music. Makeba's song was not the only one the dance inspired. Its melody was based on an instrumental, &quot;Phatha Phatha&quot;, by Shumi Ntutu and Isaac Nkosi, itself based on &quot;Noma Kumnyama&quot; by Alson Mkhize. Dorothy Masuka's popular &quot;Ei Yow Phata Phata&quot; of 1956 was a distinctly different song, though Masuka later recorded Makeba's version and claimed to have written it.</p><p>Makeba first recorded &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a>&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> with her girl group the Skylarks, in either 1956 or 1959. In 1967, after building a successful career in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, she recorded it again with Jerry Ragovoy producing, adding a spoken part in English, and Ragovoy was then billed as co-writer of words and music. Released on her album of the same name and as a single, with &quot;Malayisha&quot; on the other side, it reached No. 12 on the Billboard chart on 25 November 1967, and it is considered by many to be her signature hit.</p><p>Makeba returned to the song throughout her life: a duet with Chayanne in 1988, a new recording for her album Welela in 1990, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a> 2000&quot; on her album Homeland. On the night she died, she performed &quot;Pata Pata&quot; just before she collapsed on stage. The song has been recorded by many other artists, from the Supremes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> Puente and Manu Dibango to Thalía, Angélique Kidjo and Pink Martini, and has been used in films, television series and advertisements.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A dance song about a township dance; its mood is joyful and inviting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="600133" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Miriam-makeba-pata-pata-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="lNeP3hrm__k" data-title="Pata Pata: Miriam Makeba on The Ed Sullivan Show (1967)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Miriam Makeba on The Ed Sullivan Show (1967)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Miriam Makeba Official Channel. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lNeP3hrm__k" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Lynn Taitt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rocksteady instrumental</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Porfi Jiménez)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Wilson Simonal)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata Jala Jala Boogaloo (El Gran Combo de Puerto Rico)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">T.C.B. (the Supremes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording for the soundtrack album and television broadcast</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tape Tape (Sylvie Vartan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980</span><span class="sg-chain-h">French version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Chayanne with Miriam Makeba)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1988</span><span class="sg-chain-h">duet</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Manu Dibango)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata (Thalía)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2000</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording on Arrasando</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yamboo</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2001</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the chorus used for their song's refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Just Dance 3 (African Ladies)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover version for the video game</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Milk &amp; Sugar feat. Miriam Makeba</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">remix</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Phatha Phatha (Shumi Ntutu and Isaac Nkosi)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody is based on it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Noma Kumnyama (Alson Mkhize)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of &quot;Phatha Phatha&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pata Pata dance of the Johannesburg shebeens</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">mid-1950s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the song is named for and inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
